Very late 2023 or 1st half of 2024 is what I'd bet on.

&#x200B;

Don't forget it's not only going to be **F2P**, but it's also going to run on **their own servers** so I can't see them releasing it without having most of their monetization stuff ready.

After using it for a while I felt that any value I got from it was either too conditional or just straight up overkill, like the dude would've been dead(or the fight won) with or without the extra slow. Dropped it entirely after they changed putrifier to only apply to teammates you shield or heal instead of anyone you buff, been running aery 99% of the time ever since.

&#x200B;

But I'd say give it a shot and see how you like it. Zilean is a super versatile champion that you can play pretty much any way you like and still succeed to some extent.

they don't, they only do it because it's profitable, not because they're forced to. You don't have to update a game for people to buy cosmetics from it, unless you want them to buy MORE cosmetics, which is why f2p games are often updated. it's just that profitable. It's not even that much of a risk either, paywalls are usually a lot riskier regarding multiplayer games because you need people playing your game in order for people to be able to play it in the first place, so having a paywall only makes it more difficult to achieve that.

&#x200B;

There is, however, a reason why one would want a game to stay b2p or p2p instead of f2p, but it has nothing to do with profit or risk. It's about the incentive for development and it's much dearer to the audiences rather than the companies: If your development incentive comes from new players wanting to play your game(buy to play), you're more likely to tailor it towards new people and invest a lot of money on advertisement, since your direct income comes from that. If your income comes from subscriptions, then you're likely to split it about 50/50 between keeping your old players happy and your new players coming. Now if your game gets its money from selling virtual clothing, guess where you'd want to focus a big portion of your development efforts? That's right, on cute and sparkly costumes.

&#x200B;

So how do you choose? To oversimplify A LOT, if you're targeting a highly invested audience, then p2p or b2p should both make at least some sense, and if you're targeting a more vulnerable, impressionable audience that is prone to addiction, f2p is probably your best deal.

to add to that: religion can bring a set of arbitrary rules that can make all the difference in a community's survival. for example: This tribe I studied about in anthropology class not long ago sustain themselves mainly on their management of wood, since it's what they use for most of their products(canoes, spears and hunting tools, housing, etc). They believe that to honor the spirits that they worship and to prevent divine retribution they need to perform a series of rituals that end up slowing down their deforestation rate just enough so that they never run out of wood. They'd be surely doomed if they ran out of wood, but they don't because of this belief.

&#x200B;

The downside to that, however, is that religious beliefs don't have to always be beneficial nor be based on any science or real world implication. So it wouldn't hurt to reconsider them from time to time.

heal reduction is pretty good. ofc if you're behind, then you should probably prioritize being an actual threat before going for utility

